WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.968
Slow your scroll.

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.903
Elon Musk said over the weekend

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.305
that Twitter will limit,

00:00:05.305 --> 00:00:06.406
at least temporarily,

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:08.274
the number of posts you can read.

00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:09.242
He claims it's an effort

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:09.843
to address

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:12.045
extreme levels of data scraping,

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:13.713
so verified accounts

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:15.015
limited to 10,000 posts

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:15.482
per day,

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:17.951
unverified accounts 1000 per day.

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:19.085
And this was revealed

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:20.387
after thousands of users

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:22.022
complained that they were unable

00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:23.757
or had trouble accessing the site.

00:00:23.757 --> 00:00:24.691
Many reported

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:25.592
getting messages

00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:27.794
that they had exceeded their rate limit.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:30.997
So what is behind this latest turmoil

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:31.698
for the company?

00:00:31.698 --> 00:00:32.966
CNN's senior

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:35.168
media analyst Sarah Fischer joins us.

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:36.836
She's also senior media reporter

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:38.271
at Axios.

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:40.306
And Sara, what gives what now with him?

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:43.143
There's always something, Audie,

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:45.779
that was a dull day in Elon Musk land.

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:46.279
You know,

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:47.614
I think this is a response

00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:49.682
to the rise of a lot of big A.I.

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:50.550
platforms

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.951
that are starting to scrape

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:53.820
big social media platforms

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.688
for data to help train their algorithms.

00:00:55.688 --> 00:00:56.790
You saw Reddit

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:57.690
a few weeks ago

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:58.558
said that they would start

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:00.760
to charging developers

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.762
for some of that back and to access.

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:03.763
They got a lot of pushback

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:04.831
from their community.

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:06.066
It seems like Elon Musk

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:07.700
is trying to do something similar.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.002
But the difference, Audie, is

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:10.136
that this is going to have

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:11.171
a huge impact

00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:12.572
on the everyday users

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:13.907
experience with Twitter.

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:14.808
You saw some people

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:16.476
tweeting frustration yesterday,

00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:17.877
ironically tweeting frustration

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:18.711
about Twitter.

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:19.412
That they weren't able

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:19.979
to get severe

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:21.514
weather updates, critical news

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:22.549
and information

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:23.917
because they were being limited

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:24.818
to the number of posts

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:25.919
that they were able to view.

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:27.353
Now, Elon Musk has not said

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:28.988
how long he's going to implement

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:30.690
this temporary crackdown,

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:31.925
but I do know that it's going

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:33.493
to be frustrating for advertisers

00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:34.694
because they're reliant

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:35.895
on have the usage

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:37.831
in order to fulfill their ad campaigns,

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:39.666
make sure they have enough people

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:41.434
watching and reading tweets

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:42.836
in order to insert their ads

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:43.670
between them,

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:44.737
theoretically reliant

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:45.572
on them for business.

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:46.973
But one quick fact check,

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:47.874
what's this about

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:50.110
maybe him not paying his bills.

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:52.812
Can you talk about what's going on there?

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:54.214
There have been so many reports,

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:55.682
whether it's been submitted

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:58.351
to B2B vendors or its licensing or its

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:00.587
leases, if you will, that Twitter

00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:02.222
has struggled to pay its bills.

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:03.056
And that's because Twitter

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:03.756
is really struggling

00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:04.858
with profitability right now.

00:02:04.858 --> 00:02:06.025
Now, to be honest,

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:06.860
Twitter has always struggled

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:07.560
with profitability

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.662
even before Elon Musk took over.

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:10.563
But the difference is

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:11.498
that they are not

00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:12.665
a publicly traded company.

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:14.167
They do not need to be transparent

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:15.335
about their finances,

00:02:15.335 --> 00:02:16.269
what money they're bringing in

00:02:16.269 --> 00:02:17.537
and what money they're spending

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:18.571
And so it seems like

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:19.205
the company is

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:20.807
just in this sort of chaotic spot

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:22.876
where it's trying to stay afloat,

00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:24.644
but it's not necessarily doing it

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:26.246
in the most transparent way.

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:27.647
We keep getting reports

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:29.782
not also just about not paying bills,

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:31.351
but they're still in litigation

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:32.452
with former employees

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:34.521
about paying out severance, etc.

00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:35.622
So expect

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:36.456
Twitter to continue

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:36.689
to be

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:38.691
in the sort of financial chaos

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:39.759
until maybe they

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:40.927
can turn the platform around.

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:42.595
But I'm still skeptical, Audie,

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:43.830
even though they have

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:44.197
put in

00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:45.498
a new chief executive,

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:47.934
this platform continues to be chaos.

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:49.169
As I cover it.

00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:50.370
All right, Sara, stay with us.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.839
I want to bring Shelby and Earl back in.

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:54.774
You're so jealous that you didn't write

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:56.409
the program

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:59.012
is broadcast worthy to be right there.

00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:00.480
I think

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:02.015
why I love listening to Sarah talk about

00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:03.082
this is because I'm fascinated

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:04.150
by the business story.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:06.052
Less, less on the myopic

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:07.854
like Navel-gazing

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:09.489
people being angry about their tweets

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:10.523
not being available

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:11.658
and all that sort of stuff.

00:03:11.658 --> 00:03:13.459
But from a business perspective,

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:14.661
they bring in Linda Marino,

00:03:14.661 --> 00:03:15.929
a big time executive.

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:17.130
It's supposed to juice up their

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:17.897
advertising and,

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:19.165
you know, I'm

00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:21.701
asking Elon Musk, how does this end?

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:24.504
Well, it's not going to be pretty.

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:25.605
At this point,

00:03:25.605 --> 00:03:27.974
the estimate is that under Elon Musk,

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:28.708
the company has lost

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:29.676
two thirds of its value.

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:31.344
He paid $44 billion for it.

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:32.979
It's worth something like 50 billion.

00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:34.280
And that's not good. Right?

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:35.248
Right. Right, right.

00:03:35.248 --> 00:03:35.915
Not good.

00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:37.050
It's not going to be

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.152
the number to go in the other direction.

00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:41.454
The fact that advertisers

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:42.822
and important users

00:03:42.822 --> 00:03:44.057
like the National Weather Service

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:45.825
and others are saying like this,

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:47.627
this may not be for us

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:48.361
the algorithm

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:51.664
is it still sort of promotes and favors,

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:53.333
you know, kind of anger and division

00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:54.200
and outrage,

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:54.667
making it

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.003
a much less pleasant user experience.

00:03:57.003 --> 00:03:59.105
For everybody who's left on it.

00:03:59.105 --> 00:03:59.839
And then this idea

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:01.074
that you're going to get out of it

00:04:01.074 --> 00:04:02.642
by paying eight bucks,

00:04:02.642 --> 00:04:04.577
which has just been roundly rejected,

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:06.579
they've got very, very few subscribers

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:08.381
even among their daily users.

00:04:08.381 --> 00:04:10.617
So as a business,

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:12.952
it just seems to be falling apart.

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:13.853
Now, it's it's

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:15.488
got a lot of sort of potential.

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:17.457
It's got a lot of residual affection.

00:04:17.457 --> 00:04:19.459
It's got a lot of users apolitical tool.

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:19.726
Right.

00:04:19.726 --> 00:04:21.294
We have 20, 24 coming up.

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:22.128
I mean, do campaigns

00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:22.862
think they're going to

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:24.030
still be using Twitter?

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:24.697
Yeah, I think well,

00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:26.666
I think the big thing is

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:28.601
if it was just this change,

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:29.902
it would be a huge change.

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:31.771
But given how much Elon Musk

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:33.172
has already changed the platform,

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:33.873
like I know

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:35.041
personally, I feel like

00:04:35.041 --> 00:04:36.676
the platform is a little bit

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:37.477
less reliable

00:04:37.477 --> 00:04:41.881
now in terms of me finding verified news

00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:43.916
that has been fact-checked

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:47.220
and is legitimate And so I think

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.121
in that sense,

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:49.989
it's a little bit less of a priority

00:04:49.989 --> 00:04:50.690
for campaigns.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:51.391
I mean, Donald Trump

00:04:51.391 --> 00:04:52.558
is still not on the platform,

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:53.559
for example.

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:54.961
I do think

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:56.262
pre Elon Musk,

00:04:56.262 --> 00:04:58.231
he would have more of a reason

00:04:58.231 --> 00:04:59.432
to join it.

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:00.500
Sir, with the time we have left,

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:02.368
I mean, from a business perspective,

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:03.269
from a one day

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:04.504
Carino perspective,

00:05:04.504 --> 00:05:05.371
do that

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:06.673
when you talk to both people

00:05:06.673 --> 00:05:07.206
inside the company,

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:08.274
but also analysts outside?

00:05:08.274 --> 00:05:10.376
Is there a pathway that exists here

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:11.644
to kind of get them out of this

00:05:11.644 --> 00:05:12.645
just constant

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:15.815
state of tumult and seeming collapse,

00:05:16.049 --> 00:05:17.517
followed by resuscitation,

00:05:17.517 --> 00:05:19.719
followed by some new

00:05:19.719 --> 00:05:21.521
strategy that I can't figure out

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:23.656
there is a pathway.

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:23.890
One of

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:24.857
the things that you're going to hear

00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:25.825
internally a lot

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:27.560
is that Linda brings assurance

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:28.695
to the talent inside.

00:05:28.695 --> 00:05:29.562
There's still some people

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:30.630
who are great salespeople,

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:31.831
who are great engineers

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:32.298
that want

00:05:32.298 --> 00:05:33.733
just guidance and direction

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:34.934
and some assurance that things aren't

00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:36.035
going to change that much.

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:37.103
And they're hoping that Linda

00:05:37.103 --> 00:05:38.805
will bring some consistency.

00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:40.273
But from the outside world,

00:05:40.273 --> 00:05:41.541
the perspective of the advertisers

00:05:41.541 --> 00:05:42.275
and marketers,

00:05:42.275 --> 00:05:43.543
I just spent a good week

00:05:43.543 --> 00:05:43.943
with them

00:05:43.943 --> 00:05:46.412
at a big advertising festival overseas.

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:48.181
And the thing that you keep hearing is

00:05:48.181 --> 00:05:50.249
that Twitter is still the most relevant

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:52.185
public square for political discourse.

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:53.186
You have media,

00:05:53.186 --> 00:05:54.087
which is the parent company

00:05:54.087 --> 00:05:55.254
that Instagram and Facebook

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:56.923
that's trying to build out a rival.

00:05:56.923 --> 00:05:57.957
You have small budding

00:05:57.957 --> 00:05:59.959
rivals like Mastodon and Blue Sky.

00:06:00.093 --> 00:06:01.627
But at the end of the day, Twitter

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:03.162
is still the biggest public square

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:03.663
in the world.

00:06:03.663 --> 00:06:05.732
And so if Elon Musk can figure this out,

00:06:05.932 --> 00:06:06.999
there's definitely a product

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:09.068
case to be made for it being relevant.

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:10.770
I'm just not quite sure, quite frankly,

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.271
if he's going to be able to do it

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:14.340
and keep Linda Guarino at his side

00:06:14.941 --> 00:06:16.342
and she doesn't get too frustrated

00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:17.510
and just leave.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:17.810
Yeah.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:18.511
It's fascinating to

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:19.379
watch it all play out.

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:20.313
Sara, thanks as always

00:06:20.313 --> 00:06:21.114
